From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] phy: allwinner: A733 combo-PHY -- bracket the DP rate table so a real consumer can call it

`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-rate-2700` added a733_combophy_configure(), the phy_ops.configure slot that writes the
163-word PMA rate table for DisplayPort. Its own header said the quiet part:
"nothing in this tree calls phy_configure()". That stayed true on the real
alt-mode path -- dwc3 uses init/power_on/set_mode only, and the only caller was
the debugfs instrumentation of a DP debug patch this series does not carry ("the
debug patch" below), behind CONFIG_PHY_SUN60I_A733_COMBO_DP_BRINGUP, default n,
i.e. test kernels only.

WHAT THAT COST, MEASURED
------------------------

Two things, both on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-18:

  - Without the table, FOUR lanes never train. Two do. With the debug patch
    supplying the missing call, four lanes reach CR in one round and EQ in one
    round, 77 77 01 01, all four symbol-error counters valid at zero, held over
    20 s. The PMA diff
    that found it: 26 registers differing against the vendor's live link, among
    them idx 0xC010 = 0x51D9 against our 0x5100 -- the register `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-rate-2700`'s own header
    names as the DP value against the USB3 one.
  - Without it, no rate change is possible at all. RBR fails at ANY lane count,
    including on lanes that manage HBR. Slower should be easier, so that is not
    signal integrity: only LINK_BW_SET and DPCD 0x100 moved while the PMA stayed
    at 2.7 Gbps.

So the "caller problem" is not an architectural preference. It is the blocker.

THIS PATCH IS THE PROVIDER HALF
-------------------------------

It does not add the caller -- `drm-a733-dp-configure-the-main-link-phy` does that, in the DisplayPort transmitter
driver, which is where mainline and the vendor both put it. What it does is make
phy_configure() safe and complete enough to BE called from there:

  1. the rate and lane writes now sit inside `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence`'s reset bracket;
  2. the "is this PHY DP's" gate moves off phy->attrs.mode, which a real
     consumer cannot set without lying to dwc3;
  3. phy_ops.validate is implemented, so a consumer's rate ladder can ask
     before it acts;
  4. phy_init() is required rather than assumed.

1. THE BRACKET -- THE ONE THAT IS LOAD-BEARING
----------------------------------------------

The vendor writes the PMA rate table with the whole link held in reset:
combo0_dp_set_rate() calls combo0_dp_phy_reset(combo0, false) before the table
(bsp/drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c:901) and combo0_dp_phy_reset(combo0,
true) after it (:1613). Read at source level, those two are exactly what `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence`
ported: the `false` arm writes LINK_CTRL0 = 0, top_combo_reg + 0x4 = 0 and
LINK_CTRL3 = 0 with a 1 ms settle, which is a733_combophy_dp_teardown() line for
line; the `true` arm is the IDDQ clear plus the 0x001 -> 0x101 -> 0x131
staircase and the three handshakes, which is a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq().

Taking the 163 values without that sequence is not the sequence, and the price is
recorded: writing the table by hand into a LIVE PHY on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-18 ended in
an `Asynchronous SError Interrupt` and a power-cycle. Nothing persistent was
written; the lesson was.

The bracket therefore goes HERE, in the provider, once -- not in each caller,
where the next one forgets it. That also fixes the deviation the debug patch documented
against itself: it reaches phy_configure() after `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence`'s staircase has
already released the link, and its header says so and says the order "becomes
`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-rate-2700`'s business" when a bracket lands. This is that bracket. The debug patch needs no
change; the extra staircase run is idempotent, because `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence` writes absolute
values.

And it is what makes a rate change possible at all: on this PHY a rate change IS
a teardown and a rebuild. A consumer that moved LINK_BW_SET and DPCD 0x100 while
the PMA stayed put is precisely the RBR failure above.

Ordering inside the bracket: lane word first, rate table last. That is the order
the vendor reaches -- its .configure runs combo0_dp_set_lanes() before
combo0_dp_set_rate() (:2381,:2385) -- with one deliberate difference, below.

2. THE GATE MOVES TO dp_active
------------------------------

`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-rate-2700` refused unless phy->attrs.mode == PHY_MODE_DP. That check cannot survive a
real consumer, and the reason is not style:

  - attrs.mode is CONSUMER state. phy_set_mode_ext() records it even for a
    provider with no .set_mode (drivers/phy/phy-core.c), and dwc3 sets it to a
    USB mode on this very phy -- one struct phy, two consumers, because COMB0 is
    one serdes.
  - So a DisplayPort driver could only pass the check by writing PHY_MODE_DP over
    dwc3's record behind its back. The debug patch does exactly that and has to
    snapshot and restore it; a shipping driver should not have to.

cp->dp_active is this driver's own answer to the same question and a stricter
one: it is set only by the Type-C mux callback, only for the four-lane DP-only
pin assignments C and E, and only after phy_init() has configured the PMA. It
cannot be set from outside the driver at all. That is what now stops a stray
phy_configure() from writing the DP rate table over a live 10 Gbps link.

3. phy_ops.validate
-------------------

New. It shares the single function that decides what is implementable and touches
no register, which is what include/linux/phy/phy.h demands of the slot: "It must
not change any actual configuration of the PHY, so calling it as many times as
deemed fit by the consumer must have no side effect."

Its purpose is rate fallback. A consumer walking a rate ladder asks first and
skips a rate we have no table for, instead of discovering it by tearing a working
link down and failing to rebuild it.

4. phy_init() IS REQUIRED, NOT ASSUMED
--------------------------------------

phy.h on phy_ops.configure: "phy_init() must have been called on the phy."
cp->inited is this driver's answer, and it is now checked with its own message
(-EAGAIN), because without it there is no PMA for PMA_CMN_READY to come up on.

WHERE THE VENDOR DRIVER DIFFERS HERE, AND WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
--------------------------------------------------------------

The vendor's ARCHITECTURE on this path matches what this tree
uses: it registers a real phy_ops with .configure/.validate taking union phy_configure_opts_dp, and
its DisplayPort driver is a phy consumer that calls phy_configure()
(bsp/drivers/drm/sunxi_device/sunxi_edp.c:604-641). We take that shape. The
differences are in the details, and each one below was read in the vendor source:

| vendor | ours |
|---|---|
| combo0_dp_set_rate() has `case 8100: break;` -- writes no table at all, then records 8100 as the current rate and returns 0. HBR3 is "configured" while the PMA holds the previous rate | one rate is implemented and every other is refused BY NAME, with the number that was asked for in the message |
| its .validate returns 0 for every link rate, including that 8100, and only limits the lane count in the two-lane pin assignments | .validate answers the same question .configure will answer, from the same function, so the two cannot drift |
| returns a bare `-1` from combo0_dp_set_rate() and combo0_dp_set_lanes() | -EOPNOTSUPP for a rate with no table, -EINVAL for a lane count the PMA word cannot encode, -EAGAIN before phy_init() |
| writes the PMA lane word into a LIVE PMA, outside any bracket (its own set_lanes brackets only the re-configure, then writes 0x4011 after the release) | inside the bracket, with the rate table. A PMA write into a live PHY is what cost a board an SError; nothing says this word is exempt |
| combo0_dp_set_ssc() sets ssc_en = 1 and has no else, so SSC can never be turned off again once asked for | SSC is refused outright until the ssc_en arm of the table is ported -- one state, not a latch |
| overloads phy_set_mode_ext()'s submode with a private bitfield (orientation OR state << 2 OR hpd << 6), so a mainline-shaped consumer calling phy_set_mode_ext(PHY_MODE_DP, PHY_SUBMODE_DP) would be read as "orientation NONE, typec state SAFE, HPD 0" and tear the personality down | submode keeps the meaning phy-dp.h gives it. Orientation and pin assignment arrive through the Type-C switch and mux callbacks, which is where TCPM puts them |
| never calls phy_init() on the combo PHY at all -- edp_phy_init() inits only aux_phy and dp_phy, and edp_phy_exit() carries the comment "// ignore combo phy, leave it until other module configure it" (sunxi_drm_edp.c:826-843) | phy_init() is required and checked; the consumer takes its own reference (`drm-a733-dp-configure-the-main-link-phy`) |

Not answered by this patch, and not pretended otherwise: the vendor's swing and
pre-emphasis path (combo0_dp_set_sw_pre) is still not ported, so set_voltages is
refused. Link training cannot complete without it, and the defect list for the
training path is owed by the patch that
implements it, not by this one.

WHAT IS NOT TOUCHED
-------------------

Not one line of the USB path. a733_combophy_reset_seq(), a733_combophy_init(),
a733_combophy_exit(), a733_combophy_param_config() and the PMA tables `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`
landed are unchanged. Every line added or moved here is behind the set_rate /
set_lanes flags of a DP configure request, and behind cp->dp_active, which a
board that never enters a DP pin assignment never sets.

The two forward declarations are the only structural change: `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence`'s bracket
functions are defined after `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-rate-2700`'s table in this file, so .configure cannot see
them. Declaring them costs six lines; moving 130 lines of `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence` would bury the
change this patch actually makes.

WHAT IT OWES
------------

  1. The four-lane result, reproduced WITHOUT CONFIG_PHY_SUN60I_A733_COMBO_DP_BRINGUP.
     Observable: with a sink on J4 and the DP node's `link` file used (`drm-a733-dp-configure-the-main-link-phy`),
     PMA idx 0xC010 reads 0x51D9 (32-bit read at 0x06C80000 + (0xC010 << 1) =
     0x06C98020 shows it mirrored, 0x51D951D9), LINK_CTRL0 0x06C01000 reads
     0x00000131 with a normal plug, LINK_STUS0 0x06C01900 reads 0x00000111,
     DP_RAWS_TXDW 0x06C06004 reads 0x00000FF1, and DPCD 0x202/0x203 read 0x77
     0x77 with 0x204 bit0 and 0x205 bit0 set after training by hand.
     Falsified by: four lanes failing to train where the debug patch made them
     train on 2026-08-18, or by the SError returning -- which would say the
     bracket is not what the debug instrumentation's success rested on.
  2. A SUPERSPEED RE-PROOF. RST_BUS_SERDES is shared, this is the driver that
     owns COMB0, and the node the DP transmitter now references (`arm64-dts-a733-dp-main-link-phy`) is the one
     HW-proven at 10 Gbps. Observable: on a boot with a DP sink NOT attached,
     `lsusb -t` still shows a SuperSpeed device at 10000M and nothing in the DP
     path has run. The negative control is the important half: with no DP pin
     assignment entered, cp->dp_active is false and every phy_configure() is
     refused with a message, so LINK_CTRL0 must stay 0x00000111.
  3. A rate OTHER than 2700 Mb/s still cannot be applied, and that is now an
     honest refusal instead of a silent no-op. Rate fallback becomes real when a
     second PMA table is ported; the mechanism it needs is here.

Built only by the automated build service, not hardware-proven: no kernel was built locally for this patch;
it applies at fuzz 0 on the full series.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
--- a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
@@ -820,6 +820,25 @@
 #define  PMA_DP_LANE_CFG_2LANE		0x010C
 #define  PMA_DP_LANE_CFG_1LANE		0x010E
 
+/* The only DP link rate this driver has a PMA table for. Anything else is
+ * refused by name rather than accepted and silently not applied -- the vendor's
+ * combo0_dp_set_rate() has `case 8100: break;`, which writes no table at all,
+ * records 8100 as the current rate and returns success.
+ */
+#define DP_LINK_RATE_HBR_MBPS		2700
+
+/*
+ * `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence`'s DP reset bracket, which lives further down this file. The vendor writes
+ * the PMA rate table with the whole link held in reset and releases it
+ * afterwards -- combo0_dp_set_rate() calls combo0_dp_phy_reset(false) before the
+ * table (sunxi-cadence-combophy.c:901) and combo0_dp_phy_reset(true) after it
+ * (:1613), and those two are exactly a733_combophy_dp_teardown() and
+ * a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq(). Declared rather than moved: shifting 130 lines
+ * of `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence` would bury the change this patch actually makes.
+ */
+static void a733_combophy_dp_teardown(struct a733_combophy *cp);
+static int a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq(struct a733_combophy *cp);
+
 /*
  * The two non-PMA writes the vendor performs before the PMA body, kept in that
  * order. Both are confirmed by the same capture, from the link window rather
@@ -865,34 +884,64 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * Is this rate/lane request one the driver can carry out? Split out of
+ * .configure so that .validate can answer the same question with no side
+ * effects, which is what phy.h requires of it: "It must not change any actual
+ * configuration of the PHY, so calling it as many times as deemed fit by the
+ * consumer must have no side effect."
+ *
+ * That matters for rate fallback specifically. A consumer walking a rate ladder
+ * can ask here first and skip a rate we have no table for, instead of finding
+ * out by tearing a working link down and failing to rebuild it.
+ */
+static int a733_combophy_dp_check(const struct phy_configure_opts_dp *dp)
+{
+	if (dp->set_rate) {
+		if (dp->link_rate != DP_LINK_RATE_HBR_MBPS || dp->ssc)
+			return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+	}
+
+	if (dp->set_lanes) {
+		if (dp->lanes != 1 && dp->lanes != 2 && dp->lanes != 4)
+			return -EINVAL;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
  * phy_ops.configure is how a DisplayPort controller asks for a link rate and a
- * lane count. Nothing in this tree calls it yet -- dwc3 does not, it uses
- * init/power_on/set_mode only -- so the USB3 path is unchanged by this patch.
+ * lane count, and on the real alt-mode path that controller is the DP
+ * transmitter at 0x05740000 -- a phy consumer named in the DT, the shape both
+ * mainline and the vendor use (drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-dp.c,
+ * drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_ctrl.c; vendor sunxi_edp.c:604-620). dwc3 never
+ * calls it: it uses init/power_on/set_mode only, so the SuperSpeed path does not
+ * reach a single line below.
  *
- * What is implemented is the rate table and the lane count, and nothing else:
- * the swing/pre-emphasis tables (combo0_dp_set_sw_pre) and the DP reset bracket
- * (combo0_dp_phy_reset, which the vendor runs before and after the rate write)
- * are separate patches. So this cannot complete a DP link on its own, and it
- * says so by refusing rather than by half-configuring.
+ * THE RATE AND LANE WRITES SIT INSIDE `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence`'s RESET BRACKET. The vendor's
+ * combo0_dp_set_rate() writes the table between combo0_dp_phy_reset(false) and
+ * (true), i.e. with LINK_CTRL0, LINK_CTRL3 and DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_CTRL zeroed and
+ * the staircase re-run afterwards. Taking the 163 values without that sequence
+ * is not the sequence: doing it by hand into a live PHY on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-18
+ * ended in an asynchronous SError and a power-cycle. So the bracket is here,
+ * once, where every caller gets it -- rather than in each caller, where the next
+ * one forgets it.
+ *
+ * It is also what makes a rate change possible at all. A rate change IS a link
+ * teardown and rebuild on this PHY; a consumer that changed LINK_BW_SET and DPCD
+ * 0x100 while the PMA stayed at 2.7 Gbps is what an earlier
+ * measurement found: RBR fails at any lane count.
+ *
+ * What is still not implemented, and is refused rather than half-applied: the
+ * swing/pre-emphasis tables (combo0_dp_set_sw_pre), and every link rate except
+ * 2700 Mb/s.
  */
 static int a733_combophy_configure(struct phy *phy,
 				   union phy_configure_opts *opts)
 {
 	struct a733_combophy *cp = phy_get_drvdata(phy);
 	struct phy_configure_opts_dp *dp = &opts->dp;
-	int ret = 0;
-
-	/* COMB0 carries SuperSpeed or DP and never both, and this driver brings
-	 * it up for USB. Refusing unless the consumer has claimed the PHY for DP
-	 * is what stops a stray phy_configure() from writing the DP rate table
-	 * over a live 10 Gbps link: phy_set_mode_ext() records the mode even for
-	 * a driver with no .set_mode (drivers/phy/phy-core.c), and dwc3 sets it
-	 * to one of the USB modes.
-	 */
-	if (phy->attrs.mode != PHY_MODE_DP) {
-		dev_warn(cp->dev, "phy_configure() while not in DP mode\n");
-		return -EINVAL;
-	}
+	int ret;
 
 	if (dp->set_voltages) {
 		dev_warn_once(cp->dev,
@@ -900,27 +949,129 @@
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 	}
 
+	if (!dp->set_rate && !dp->set_lanes)
+		return 0;
+
+	/* -EOPNOTSUPP is the rate, -EINVAL the lane count. Each is named on its
+	 * own, and only the field the consumer actually set is printed: a
+	 * union phy_configure_opts is routinely left partly uninitialised by
+	 * its callers (dw-dp.c declares it on the stack and fills three
+	 * fields), so printing dp->lanes for a rate-only request would be
+	 * printing whatever was on that stack.
+	 */
+	ret = a733_combophy_dp_check(dp);
+	if (ret == -EOPNOTSUPP) {
+		dev_warn(cp->dev,
+			 "DP link rate %u Mb/s%s refused: only %u Mb/s without SSC has a PMA table\n",
+			 dp->link_rate, dp->ssc ? " with SSC" : "",
+			 DP_LINK_RATE_HBR_MBPS);
+		return ret;
+	}
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(cp->dev,
+			 "DP lane count %u refused: the PMA lane word encodes 1, 2 or 4\n",
+			 dp->lanes);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
 	mutex_lock(&cp->lock);
 
-	if (dp->set_rate) {
-		if (dp->link_rate != 2700 || dp->ssc) {
-			dev_warn(cp->dev,
-				 "only DP link rate 2700 Mb/s without SSC is implemented (asked %u Mb/s%s)\n",
-				 dp->link_rate, dp->ssc ? ", SSC" : "");
-			ret = -EOPNOTSUPP;
-			goto out;
-		}
-		a733_combophy_dp_rate_2700(cp);
+	/* phy.h on phy_ops.configure: "phy_init() must have been called on the
+	 * phy". cp->inited is this driver's own answer to that, and without it
+	 * there is no PMA for PMA_CMN_READY to come up on. The vendor never
+	 * calls phy_init() on this PHY at all -- its eDP driver takes
+	 * phy_power_on() and phy_configure() on a combo phy it leaves to
+	 * "other module" to initialise (sunxi_drm_edp.c:826-843, and the
+	 * comment in edp_phy_exit() says so in those words).
+	 */
+	if (!cp->inited) {
+		dev_warn(cp->dev,
+			 "phy_configure() before phy_init(): the PMA is not configured, refusing\n");
+		ret = -EAGAIN;
+		goto out;
 	}
 
-	if (dp->set_lanes)
+	/* COMB0 carries SuperSpeed or DP and never both. dp_active is set only
+	 * by the Type-C mux callback, and only for the four-lane DP-only pin
+	 * assignments, after phy_init() -- so it is this driver's own answer to
+	 * "do the lanes belong to DisplayPort", and it is what stops a stray
+	 * phy_configure() from writing the DP rate table over a live 10 Gbps
+	 * link.
+	 *
+	 * It replaces a check on phy->attrs.mode, which could not survive a real
+	 * consumer: attrs.mode is CONSUMER state (phy_set_mode_ext() records it
+	 * even for a driver with no .set_mode, drivers/phy/phy-core.c), dwc3
+	 * sets it to a USB mode on this very phy, and a DP driver that wrote
+	 * PHY_MODE_DP into it would be overwriting dwc3's record behind its
+	 * back to satisfy a check the PHY can answer for itself.
+	 */
+	if (!cp->dp_active) {
+		dev_warn(cp->dev,
+			 "phy_configure() while the lanes are not DisplayPort's -- no DP pin assignment has been entered\n");
+		ret = -EINVAL;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	/* The bracket. Lane count first and the rate table last, which is the
+	 * order the vendor reaches (combo0_dp_set_lanes() then
+	 * combo0_dp_set_rate() in its .configure) -- with one difference: the
+	 * vendor writes the lane word into a LIVE PMA, outside any bracket. It
+	 * is inside ours, because a PMA write into a live PHY is what cost a
+	 * board an SError on 2026-08-18 and nothing says this word is exempt.
+	 */
+	a733_combophy_dp_teardown(cp);
+
+	if (dp->set_lanes) {
 		ret = a733_combophy_dp_set_lanes(cp, dp->lanes);
+		if (ret)
+			goto release;
+	}
+
+	if (dp->set_rate)
+		a733_combophy_dp_rate_2700(cp);
+
+release:
+	/* Always release, even if a write above failed: leaving the link in
+	 * reset would strand the PHY in a state no later call recovers, and
+	 * UM 18.2.6.8 requires POWER_STATE_REQ to return to 0 and be
+	 * re-requested, which the staircase does.
+	 */
+	if (a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq(cp) && !ret)
+		ret = -EIO;
 
 out:
 	mutex_unlock(&cp->lock);
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * phy_ops.validate. Side-effect free by construction -- it shares the one
+ * function that decides what is implementable and touches no register. Its
+ * whole purpose is that a consumer's rate or lane fallback ladder can ask
+ * before it acts.
+ *
+ * The vendor has this slot too, with a looser answer: its
+ * sunxi_cadence_combo0_dp_phy_validate() returns 0 for every rate, including
+ * the 8100 its own .configure silently does not apply, and only limits the lane
+ * count in the two-lane pin assignments. Its caller then maps -EINVAL to "pass"
+ * (sunxi_drm_edp.c:846-868), so the one case it does reject is discarded again.
+ */
+static int a733_combophy_validate(struct phy *phy, enum phy_mode mode,
+				  int submode, union phy_configure_opts *opts)
+{
+	if (mode != PHY_MODE_DP)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/* phy-dp.h defines exactly two: PHY_SUBMODE_DP and PHY_SUBMODE_EDP.
+	 * This board has no eDP panel -- the four lanes go to the Type-C
+	 * connector -- so eDP is refused rather than treated as a synonym.
+	 */
+	if (submode != PHY_SUBMODE_DP)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	return a733_combophy_dp_check(&opts->dp);
+}
+
 /* combo0_usb_phy_reset(true): reset pulse, DP-lane conv, PLL enable, the
  * POWER_STATE handshake, and the PMA_CMN_READY wait (+ one PMA nudge).
  */
@@ -1404,6 +1555,7 @@
 	.init		= a733_combophy_init,
 	.exit		= a733_combophy_exit,
 	.configure	= a733_combophy_configure,
+	.validate	= a733_combophy_validate,
 	.owner		= THIS_MODULE,
 };
 
